Chapter: Lwm2mClientBinary
  cites Net chapter NetDriver
  cites Net chapter Lwm2mClient
  cites Net chapter UdpIO
  cites Encode chapter Lwm2m
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 `Lwm2mClient` was a pure state machine with no caller outside
 `codex/test/apps/lwm2m-client`, which drives it against hand-built CoAP
 requests rather than a peer. That test proves our half answers itself, and
 `docs/PM/Active/Stories/BrotliBeatsOpus.md` is the account of why two halves
 sharing an assumption agree forever. This binary is the socket the client
 did not have: registration, the management session that follows, and
 deregistration, over UDP.

 WHAT THIS DOES NOT PROVE. There is no LwM2M server on this box, so nothing
 here has yet been answered by an implementation that reads
 OMA-TS-LightweightM2M-V1_1 instead of our `Lwm2m` chapter. Pointed at
 Leshan or any other server it becomes an interop arm; run against nothing it
 reports that the datagrams left and none came back, which is a true
 statement about the socket and no statement at all about the protocol.
 `build/coap-interop-test.ps1` is the shape the LwM2M equivalent would take.

 THE CLOCK IS THE PIT TICK, as in `coap-client`: `get-ticks` counts the
 periodic timer at roughly 18.2 Hz. The LwM2M lifetime is in seconds and
 `lwm2m-client-tick` counts calls rather than time, so this binary converts
 once, here, and passes the client whole ticks of its own.

Section: Configuration

  lw-local-mac : List Integer = net-driver-mac
  lw-local-ip : List Integer = [10, 0, 2, 15]
  lw-gateway-ip : List Integer = [10, 0, 2, 2]

  lw-local-port : Integer = 5902
  lw-server-port : Integer = 5683

  lw-poll-fuel : Integer = 400000
  lw-rounds : Integer = 400

 A lifetime of 8 with the client's own halving rule sends an Update once the
 counter falls to 4, which is inside a run of `lw-rounds` rather than a
 number nobody waits for.

  lw-lifetime : Integer = 8

  lw-registration : Lwm2mRegistration = Lwm2mRegistration {
    endpoint = "codex-probe", lifetime = lw-lifetime, binding = "U",
    objects = [lwm2m-obj-device, lwm2m-obj-server]
  }

  lw-resources : List Lwm2mRes = [
    lwm2m-res lwm2m-obj-device 0 lwm2m-res-manufacturer [67, 111, 100, 101, 120],
    lwm2m-res lwm2m-obj-device 0 lwm2m-res-model [80, 114, 111, 98, 101],
    lwm2m-res-exec lwm2m-obj-device 0 lwm2m-res-reboot,
    lwm2m-res-rw lwm2m-obj-server 0 1 [51, 48, 48]
  ]

Section: The Exchange

  lw-send-all : UdpSocket, List (List Integer), Integer -> [Network.Write] Integer
  lw-send-all (sk) (outs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length outs then 0
    else act
      sent <- udp-io-send sk (list-at outs i)
      rest <- lw-send-all sk outs (i + 1)
      sent + rest
    end

 One socket and one client carried through every round, for the reason
 `coap-client` records: the message id lives in the client, and building a
 fresh one restarts it, which RFC 7252 section 4.5 lets a server treat as a
 duplicate and answer from its cache.

 A round that received nothing still ticks, because the lifetime is what
 drives the Update and a silent server must not stop the clock.

  lw-run : UdpSocket, Lwm2mClient, Integer -> [Network.Read, Network.Write] Lwm2mClient
  lw-run (sk) (c) (rounds) =
    if rounds <= 0 then c
    else act
      r <- udp-io-recv sk lw-poll-fuel
      let step = if r.ur-got then lwm2m-client-recv c (r.ur-data)
                 else lwm2m-client-tick c
      in act
        sent <- lw-send-all sk (step.ls-out) 0
        lw-run sk (step.ls-client) (rounds - 1)
      end
    end

  lw-register : UdpSocket, Lwm2mClient -> [Network.Read, Network.Write] Lwm2mClient
  lw-register (sk) (c) =
    let step = lwm2m-client-register c
    in act
      sent <- lw-send-all sk (step.ls-out) 0
      lw-run sk (step.ls-client) lw-rounds
    end

  lw-deregister : UdpSocket, Lwm2mClient -> [Network.Read, Network.Write] Lwm2mClient
  lw-deregister (sk) (c) =
    let step = lwm2m-client-deregister c
    in act
      sent <- lw-send-all sk (step.ls-out) 0
      step.ls-client
    end

Section: Reporting

 `registered` is the discriminating cell and it is the one to read first: it
 is False until a 2.01 Created carrying Location-Path arrives, so it cannot
 be True unless a server answered. `executions` counts Execute requests the
 server sent us, which separates a server that merely accepted the
 registration from one that went on to drive the object tree.

  lw-report : Text, Lwm2mClient -> [Console.Write] Nothing
  lw-report (label) (c) = act
    print-line-uni ("lwm2m " & label
      & " registered=" & show (lwm2m-client-registered c)
      & " executions=" & show (lwm2m-client-executions c))
  end

Section: Entry

  opening : [Network.Read, Network.Write, Console.Write] Nothing = act
    let sk = udp-io-open lw-local-mac lw-local-ip lw-local-port lw-gateway-ip lw-server-port
    in let c0 = lwm2m-client-new lw-registration lw-resources
    in act
      live <- lw-register sk c0
      lw-report "session" live
      gone <- lw-deregister sk live
      lw-report "deregistered" gone
    end
  end

